Build Specification — App 35: The Argument Mapper for Students
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste. Long lists are broken into short lines.

1. What It Is
A tool that lets a student map out an argument. The student records the premises, the conclusions, the assumptions, the counterarguments, and the responses. The Argument Mapper records each argument as a graph: the premises support the conclusion, the assumptions are unstated premises, the counterarguments attack the conclusion or a premise, and the responses defend against the counterarguments. It is the practice tool for argumentation.

It reuses:

the STAG Annotation Editor (App 1)

the STAG Schema Library (App 17)

the STAG Graph Viewer (App 5)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Learning Provenance System (App 33)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for students, teachers, tutors, and anyone who teaches or learns argumentation.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of arguments from users.

Store each argument with its name, its conclusion, its premises, and its provenance.

Record the premises: the statements that support the conclusion.

Record the assumptions: the unstated premises the argument relies on.

Record the conclusion: the statement the argument tries to establish.

Record the counterarguments: the arguments that attack the conclusion or a premise.

Record the responses: the defenses against the counterarguments.

Record the argument type: deductive, inductive, abductive, analogical, causal, or other.

Record the argument strength: strong, moderate, weak, or uncertain.

Record the validity of the argument: valid, invalid, or not_assessed.

Record the soundness of the argument: sound, unsound, or not_assessed.

Record the fallacies: any logical fallacies present in the argument.

Record the student's assessment of each part.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by argument ID, name, subject, topic, argument type, strength, validity, soundness, student, date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the argument description, the premises, the conclusion, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by subject, by argument type, by strength, by validity, by student, by year.

Support exporting an argument as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing an argument from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking an argument to a Learning Provenance System record if the argument involves a learned concept.

Support linking an argument to a Physics Knowledge Library work if the argument is described in a deposited work.

Support the argument graph: a visualisation of the premises, the conclusion, the assumptions, the counterarguments, and the responses.

Support the argument comparison view: a visualisation that compares two or more arguments for the same conclusion.

Support the fallacy map: a visualisation of the fallacies present in an argument.

Support the student progress view: a visualisation of the student's argumentation skills over time.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical argument in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 arguments.

Versioned: the mapper records the STAG schema version, the argument schema version, and the mapper version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

