6. Full Argument Specification
6.1 Argument Schema
The argument schema defines the structure of an argument deposit.

It has the following fields:

argument_id. A string. Required.

argument_name. A string. Required.

argument_description. A string. Required.

subject. A string. Required.

topic. A string. Required.

conclusion. An object. Required. It contains statement and confidence.

premises. An array of premise objects. Required.

assumptions. An array of assumption objects. Optional.

counterarguments. An array of counterargument objects. Optional.

responses. An array of response objects. Optional.

argument_type. An enum. Required. The values are deductive, inductive, abductive, analogical, causal, other.

argument_strength. An enum. Required. The values are strong, moderate, weak, uncertain.

validity. An enum. Required. The values are valid, invalid, not_assessed.

soundness. An enum. Required. The values are sound, unsound, not_assessed.

fallacies. An array of fallacy objects. Optional.

student_assessments. An array of assessment objects. Optional.

student_id. A string. Required.

date_created. A string in ISO8601 format. Required.

date_last_reviewed. A string in ISO8601 format. Optional.

learning_record_reference. A string. Optional.

provenance. An object. Required.

work_reference. A string. Optional.

keywords. An array of strings. Optional.

notes. A string. Optional.

moderation_status. An enum. The values are pending, approved, rejected.

version. An integer.

supersedes. A string. Optional.

superseded_by. A string. Optional.

Each premise object has:

premise_id. A string.

statement. A string.

premise_type. An enum. The values are fact, value, policy, definition, observation, other.

confidence. An enum. The values are high, medium, low.

support. An enum. The values are direct, indirect, background.

notes. A string. Optional.

Each assumption object has:

assumption_id. A string.

statement. A string.

assumption_type. An enum. The values are factual, conceptual, normative, methodological, other.

confidence. An enum. The values are high, medium, low.

notes. A string. Optional.

Each counterargument object has:

counterargument_id. A string.

statement. A string.

target. An enum. The values are conclusion, premise, assumption, other.

target_id. A string. Optional.

counterargument_type. An enum. The values are rebuttal, undercutting, undermining, other.

strength. An enum. The values are strong, moderate, weak.

notes. A string. Optional.

Each response object has:

response_id. A string.

statement. A string.

target_counterargument_id. A string.

response_type. An enum. The values are denial, distinction, counterexample, concession, other.

strength. An enum. The values are strong, moderate, weak.

notes. A string. Optional.

Each fallacy object has:

fallacy_id. A string.

fallacy_name. A string.

fallacy_description. A string.

location. A string.

notes. A string. Optional.

Each assessment object has:

assessment_id. A string.

assessment_type. An enum. The values are self, peer, teacher.

assessment_date. A string in ISO8601 format.

assessor_id. A string.

assessment_description. A string.

rating. An enum. The values are excellent, good, adequate, weak, poor.

notes. A string. Optional.

